Chapter 2: Related Work and Collision Register

2.1 The Agentic Commerce Protocol Landscape

The protocol landscape is best understood as several distinct layers, each solving a different problem.

Agent–source context. The Model Context Protocol provides a generic tool and data channel between an agent and its source systems. The MCP 2026-07-28 revision represents a breaking redesign: it removed sessions and the initialize step, requires OAuth 2.1 for authorization servers, mandates the resource parameter per RFC 8707, and incorporates Protected Resource Metadata per RFC 9728. Implementers must follow OAuth 2.1 security best practices. MCP maintains a dedicated security-considerations document identifying attack vectors and best practices; UACN's trust-boundary discussion is intended to be compatible with that document rather than to replace it.

Agent–agent communication. Agent2Agent v1.0, released in March 2026, provides communication between autonomous agents, including cryptographically signed agent cards, multi-tenancy, version negotiation, and multi-protocol bindings. Signed agent cards provide cryptographic verification of agent identity and metadata, establishing trust before interaction across organizational boundaries. Dedicated negotiation protocols define offer exchange, mandate verification, and session lifecycle for commercial negotiation between agents representing different organizations with competing interests.

Commerce orchestration. Protocols such as the Agentic Commerce Protocol and the Universal Commerce Protocol define how agents discover products, query catalogs, and construct carts across merchant environments. UCP was released by Google in January 2026, co-developed with Shopify and backed by Walmart, Target, Wayfair, Etsy, Mastercard, Visa, and more than twenty other partners. ACP, launched by OpenAI and Stripe in September 2025, powers Instant Checkout in ChatGPT and was later narrowed in March 2026 to focus on product discovery.

Payment execution and authorization. Google's Agent Payments Protocol v0.2, donated to the FIDO Alliance on 28 April 2026, defines Checkout Mandates and Payment Mandates, including cryptographic binding between a checkout and payment authorization. AP2 uses SD-JWTs (Selective Disclosure JSON Web Tokens) for mandates, with "alg": "ES256" in the JOSE header. The protocol defines two mandate types — Checkout and Payment — each with Open and Closed stages supporting human-present and human-not-present transaction flows. The binding is via transaction_id = base64url(sha256(checkout_jwt)). AP2 defines three signed artifacts as W3C Verifiable Credentials. Mandates are verifiable digital credentials, not settlement rails. The specification is maintained in the google-agentic-commerce/AP2 repository, with standardization continuing within FIDO technical working groups.

Verifiable intent. Mastercard and Google's Verifiable Intent, open-sourced on 5 March 2026, provides a protocol-agnostic trust layer for agentic commerce that creates a tamper-resistant record of what users authorized when an AI agent acts on their behalf. Verifiable Intent uses SD-JWT delegation chains with eight machine-verifiable constraint types, from amount bounds to merchant allowlists, and is designed to work across AP2 and UCP. The specification has been donated to the Decentralized Identity Foundation and is being integrated into Mastercard's Agent Pay APIs.

Machine payments. Stripe and Tempo's Machine Payments Protocol, launched on 18 March 2026, defines session-based micropayment channels for agent budgets. MPP runs on the Tempo blockchain but is designed to be rail-agnostic and extensible, covering payment request, authorization, and settlement procedures. Visa has extended MPP to support card-based payments on its global network and operates an anchor validator on the Tempo blockchain.

Agent authentication. Infrastructure-level authentication such as Web Bot Auth and vendor trusted-agent programs allow merchants to cryptographically verify that an agent is registered, authenticated, and mandated. These build on HTTP Message Signatures (RFC 9421) and key directories.

Delegation and identity. W3C Verifiable Credentials 2.0 and Decentralized Identifiers v1.0 provide substrate for agent identity and provenance. Rail-agnostic trust layers chain an agent's authority to an accountable party, bounded by scope, spend, and time.

Zero-trust runtime verification. Lan et al. (2026) propose context-binding and consume-once mandate semantics for AP2, using dynamically generated, time-bound nonces. The framework ensures that authorization decisions are evaluated at execution time rather than assumed from static issuance properties, and reports approximately 3.8 ms verification latency at up to 10,000 transactions per second, with runtime state bounded by peak concurrency rather than history.

Adversarial analysis of AP2. Published red-teaming of AP2 reference implementations identifies two classes of attack that are directly relevant to UACN. The Branded Whisper Attack embeds adversarial instructions in product descriptions to manipulate ranking decisions. The Vault Whisper Attack crafts prompts that induce cross-user data disclosure. Both attacks produce cryptographically valid mandates — "signing the transaction but not the decision" — and the red-teaming study implemented a shopping agent on Gemini-2.5-Flash with the Agent Development Kit, reporting 100% attack success rate for indirect injection. The key finding is that cryptographic correctness does not ensure reasoning integrity: prompt injection operates entirely within the reasoning process yet directly affects transaction outcomes. UACN's tier separation is designed to mitigate, not eliminate, this class.

