Chapter 3: Trust Model and Data Classification

3.1 The Four Tiers

T1 — Verified control-plane data. Authorization records, server-enforced limits, signed payment-provider responses, and validated transaction-state transitions.

T2 — Retrieved content. Merchant descriptions, reviews, external webpages, product images, search snippets, and arbitrary documents.

T3 — LLM-generated content. Product classifications, recommendations, tool arguments, summaries, and proposed actions.

T4 — Payment and identity secrets. Card credentials, authentication secrets, payment tokens, private keys, and personally identifiable information. Consumer-held signing keys are explicitly excluded from T4 for the purposes of the control-plane custody claim; they are held in the consumer's authenticator and are not exportable.

3.2 Operational Rules

R-1. Authority is tier-bound. Only T1 may satisfy a guard that gates a state transition. T2 and T3 may inform, never authorize.

R-2. T2 is always untrusted input. Retrieved content is passed to the reasoning plane as data, never as instructions.

R-3. T3 is always a proposal. LLM output is validated against a schema before it becomes an argument to any control-plane function.

R-4. T4 never enters the reasoning plane. Consumer-held signing keys are not T4 for the purposes of this rule; they never enter any plane other than the consumer's authenticator.

R-5. Provenance is attached at the boundary.

R-6. Promotion is explicit.

R-7. Demotion is not permitted.

R-8. T1 is exposed to the reasoning plane only through a derived view. The reasoning plane may receive a sanitized projection of T1 facts — for example, a price, a delivery estimate, a merchant name — for the purpose of generating explanations. This projection is constructed by the control plane, contains only fields explicitly enumerated in a schema, never contains the merchant signature, the authorization object, or any field whose disclosure would enable forgery, and is provenance-tagged as T1-derived. The derived view does not carry authority. R-1 continues to apply: only T1 proper satisfies guards.

3.3 Formal Information-Flow Constraint

Let P_r denote the reasoning plane and P_c the control plane. Define the set of tiers visible to a component:

\mathrm{Vis}(P_r) \subseteq \{\mathrm{T1}_{\mathrm{derived}}, \mathrm{T2}, \mathrm{T3}\}, \qquad \mathrm{Vis}(P_c) \subseteq \{\mathrm{T1}, \mathrm{T2}, \mathrm{T3}, \mathrm{T4}\}

The isolation invariant is:

\forall x \in \mathrm{T4}: \; x \notin \mathrm{Vis}(P_r)

and, additionally:

\mathrm{T1}_{\mathrm{derived}} \cap \mathrm{T4} = \emptyset

This is enforced at the API boundary, the logging boundary, the tracing boundary, and the model-context boundary. Conformance is tested in tests/adversarial/.

