Chapter 4: Threat Model

4.1 Adversary Classes

We model five adversary classes.

A1 — Malicious merchant content. An adversary controls product descriptions, reviews, seller messages, or support pages. Goal: induce the agent to violate consumer constraints, exfiltrate T4, or misdirect payment.

A2 — Malicious or compromised merchant adapter. An adversary controls a merchant endpoint or its integration. Goal: bait-and-switch checkout changes, forge confirmations, or produce duplicate logical purchases. This class includes capability-vector misrepresentation: a malicious adapter may declare \kappa_{\mathrm{idem}} = 1 and behave otherwise.

A3 — Network adversary. An adversary observes or manipulates messages in transit. Goal: replay authorization, delay confirmation, or induce reconciliation ambiguity.

A4 — Compromised reasoning plane. An adversary controls the LLM or its context. Goal: emit T3 proposals that, if authorized, would produce unauthorized purchases.

A5 — Compromised control plane. An adversary controls a component of the control plane, or an insider with legitimate access abuses it. Goal: issue authorizations without consumer consent, bypass constraint filters, forge audit entries, or exfiltrate T4 from its sole legitimate holder.

A5 is not mitigated by the tier separation of Chapter 3, because the control plane is where that separation is enforced. Mitigations for A5 are structural and operational: least privilege, separation of duties between authorization signing and transaction orchestration, hardware-backed key storage, append-only audit with independent witness, and reproducible build attestation.

