Chapter 10: Architecture

10.1 Two Planes

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│            REASONING PLANE              │
│  LLM · planners · explainers            │
│  admissible: T1_derived, T2, T3         │
│  forbidden: T4                          │
└──────────────────┬──────────────────────┘
                   │ T3 proposals only
                   ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│             CONTROL PLANE               │
│  schema registry · intent validator     │
│  constraint filter · ranker             │
│  authorization manager                  │
│  order orchestrator (saga)              │
│  reconciliation · audit log             │
│  owns: T1 · sole holder of server-side T4│
└──────────────────┬──────────────────────┘
                   │ signed requests
                   ▼
      Payment provider / merchant APIs
```

10.2 Domain Model Independence

The domain model is independent of any particular LLM SDK. ChatGPT, DeepSeek, Gemini, Claude, Grok, and other models connect through adapters implementing the same structured interface. Model substitution is a testable architectural property.

10.3 Participant Capability Declaration

Each merchant adapter declares the capability vector \kappa_m. If a merchant cannot provide reliable idempotency or status reconciliation, the orchestrator marks the outcome as uncertain and does not automatically retry. The capability vector is self-declared; merchant adapter attestation is a later milestone.

10.4 Key Management

Consumer signing keys are held in the consumer's authenticator and are not exportable. The control plane does not hold them.

Control-plane signing keys are a separate concern. The design calls for:

Hardware-backed storage. Control-plane signing keys are held in an HSM or equivalent. They are not held in application memory or in configuration files.

Separation of duties. The key that signs audit entries is distinct from the key that signs payment assertions. Compromise of one does not compromise the other.

Rotation. Keys are rotated on a schedule and on suspicion of compromise. Rotation is logged.

Independent witness. The audit log is periodically anchored to an external, append-only witness (for example, a transparency log). This converts a compromised-control-plane forgery from an undetectable act into a detectable one, at the cost of latency.

Compromise recovery. The recovery procedure for a compromised control-plane key is documented, tested, and does not require the cooperation of merchants or payment providers to detect, even if it requires their cooperation to remediate. The procedure is: rotate the compromised key, re-anchor the witness, notify payment service providers, and do not retroactively invalidate past audit entries (the log is append-only). This answers the distinction between detection and remediation.

