Chapter 11: Experimental Methodology

11.1 Design

A comparison between manual shopping and an LLM-assisted UACN system would not isolate the contribution of the orchestration architecture from the contribution of the LLM. A more informative experiment uses four conditions.

Condition Workflow Intent Interface What It Measures
A Manual shopping Human-only Human-only baseline
B Conventional agent shopping Structured Structured agent baseline without UACN control plane
C Deterministic UACN Structured Value of the UACN control plane without LLM proposals
D LLM-assisted UACN Structured Incremental contribution of LLM assistance within the UACN control plane

Common intent interface. Conditions B, C, and D share the same structured intent interface. The user specifies constraints, budget, and preferences through the same form. The LLM appears only in Condition D's proposal-generation path. Without this, the interface itself would be part of the treatment and the estimands would not be identified. Condition A uses a different interface and is therefore a benchmark, not a control; this must be stated explicitly in the pre-registration.

11.2 Primary Estimands

For a task-level outcome Y:

\Delta_{\mathrm{LLM}} = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathrm{UACN}_{\mathrm{LLM}}] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathrm{UACN}_{\mathrm{deterministic}}]

\Delta_{\mathrm{UACN}} = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathrm{UACN}_{\mathrm{deterministic}}] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathrm{Conventional\_agent}]

\Delta_{\mathrm{UACN}} is a bundle effect. It measures the joint contribution of the UACN control plane — filtering, authorization, canonicalization, reconciliation, audit — and does not decompose it into individual mechanisms. Decomposition is a separate study, requiring additional conditions that vary one mechanism at a time.

These are proposed estimands. Their interpretation depends on randomized assignment, comparable interfaces, a prespecified analysis plan, and control of relevant confounders. The randomization unit is the task; participants are blinded to condition where feasible; the sample-size rationale is based on the primary outcome of hard-constraint violation rate.

11.3 Outcomes

The experiment predefines: task success and hard-constraint violation rates; total task completion time and human intervention time; incorrect or duplicate order attempts; authorization failures and recovery outcomes; total cost, including shipping and applicable fees; accessibility and participant comprehension; and adverse events and missing-data handling. Task success requires that the final order record matches the intent on all hard-constraint fields and that no constraint is violated.

11.4 Controlled Catalog

The experiment uses a controlled catalog with known ground truth before introducing real merchant integrations. A simulated order is evidence of simulator behavior, not proof of successful physical delivery.

11.5 Pre-Registration

The study publishes its protocol, randomization procedure, sample-size rationale, task definitions, statistical analysis plan, and limitations before collecting confirmatory results.

