Appendix A: Formal Protocol Integration Specifications

A.1 AP2 v0.2 Mandate Structure and UACN Extension Profile

The Agent Payments Protocol v0.2 defines five roles: Shopping Agent (SA), Credential Provider (CP), Merchant (M), Merchant Payment Processor (MPP), and Trusted Surface (TS). The Trusted Surface is the UI surface trusted to obtain informed user consent before creating a user-signed mandate. This role corresponds directly to UACN's approval ceremony.

AP2 uses SD-JWTs for both Checkout and Payment Mandates, with four mandate types across two dimensions: Open/Closed × Checkout/Payment. The Checkout Mandate is bound to the Payment Mandate via a cryptographic hash of the Checkout JWT. To prevent rainbow table attacks, the Checkout JWT must be signed using a digital signature scheme (e.g., ECDSA) and not a deterministic signature (e.g., Ed25519).

UACN's authorization object \mathcal{A} is encoded as an SD-JWT that extends the AP2 Payment Mandate with three additional selectively-disclosable claims:

\text{uacn\_p\_id} = P_{\mathrm{id}} = H(u, i, h_c)

\text{uacn\_context} = \mathrm{ctx} = H(m, h_c, P_{\mathrm{id}}, n)

\text{uacn\_capability} = \kappa_m = (\kappa_{\mathrm{idem}}, \kappa_{\mathrm{status}}, \kappa_{\mathrm{stable\_id}}, \kappa_{\mathrm{pay\_status}}, \kappa_{\mathrm{recon}})

An AP2-only verifier ignores these claims; a UACN-aware verifier consumes them. This preserves AP2 compatibility while adding UACN's orchestration guarantees.

A.2 Verifiable Intent Constraint Mapping

Mastercard and Google's Verifiable Intent uses SD-JWT delegation chains with eight machine-verifiable constraint types. The specification builds a three-layer delegation chain: issuer to user to agent. UACN's hard_constraints field maps to the VI constraint taxonomy as follows:

VI Constraint Type UACN Field Semantic Equivalence
Amount bound budget_max Maximum total spend
Currency currency ISO 4217 alphabetic code
Merchant allowlist merchant_allowlist Permitted merchants
Merchant denylist merchant_denylist Forbidden merchants
Budget cap budget_max Aggregate spend limit
Recurrence limit Not currently modeled Frequency bound
Time window delivery_deadline Temporal bound
Line items match required_attributes Product constraint

The VI delegation chain is carried in \mathcal{A} as a selectively disclosable claim. A UACN verifier evaluates the delegation chain and hard_constraints together; the VI chain provides the cryptographic authority, and UACN's constraint filter provides the deterministic enforcement.

A.3 UCP Catalog Integration

The Universal Commerce Protocol provides two capabilities relevant to UACN: Cart and Catalog. The Cart capability allows agents to add multiple items from a single retailer in one operation, supporting pre-purchase basket construction. The Catalog capability enables real-time product queries directly from retailer inventory, including variants, pricing, and stock levels.

UACN's MerchantOffer schema is constructed from the UCP Catalog response by an adapter. The adapter performs four operations:

1. Attribute extraction. Structured attributes from the UCP response are mapped to the attributes object of MerchantOffer.
2. Signature preservation. The merchant's UCP signature is preserved in the signature field.
3. Provenance assignment. Each field is tagged as T1 (signed, verified), T2 (retrieved, untrusted), or T3 (LLM-generated).
4. Free-text isolation. The description field is marked T2 and is never interpreted as instructions by the control plane.

The UCP catalog response conforms to a UCP metadata envelope that includes pricing, availability signals, and checkout links. The adapter validates the envelope against the UCP schema before constructing the MerchantOffer.

A.4 A2A v1.0 Agent Card Verification

A2A v1.0 agent cards are JCS-canonical JSON (RFC 8785) signed with a per-installation Ed25519 key (RFC 8037) as a detached JWS (RFC 7515). The card is published at a stable .well-known path and includes supportedInterfaces[], securitySchemes[], capabilities[], skills[], and signatures[].

UACN's merchant adapter verifies the agent card before accepting any agent-to-agent message:

\mathrm{Verify}_{\mathrm{agent\_card}} = \mathrm{Verify}_{\mathrm{Ed25519}}(\sigma_{\mathrm{card}}, \mathrm{JCS}(\mathrm{card})) \wedge \mathrm{Valid}(\mathrm{card}) \wedge \mathrm{now} < \mathrm{card.expires\_at}

The did field of the verified card is bound to the merchant identity m in the authorization object \mathcal{A}. A message from an agent whose card does not verify is rejected before any state transition.

A.5 MCP 2026-07-28 Authorization Profile

MCP 2026-07-28 requires OAuth 2.1 for authorization servers and mandates the resource parameter per RFC 8707 in both authorization and token requests. MCP clients must implement PKCE with the S256 code challenge method. MCP servers must validate that tokens presented to them were specifically issued for their use.

UACN's MCP server exposes a restricted tool set to the reasoning plane:

Tool Input Schema Output Schema Tier Boundary
validate_intent PurchaseIntent ValidationResult T3 → T1
search_offers IntentRef MerchantOffer[] T2 → T3
filter_offers MerchantOffer[], Constraints MerchantOffer[] T1 (control plane)
rank_offers MerchantOffer[], Preferences RankedOffers T1 (control plane)
request_approval CanonicalCheckout CeremonyHandle T1 (control plane)
submit_order Authorization SubmissionResult T1 (control plane)
query_status P_id OrderStatus T1 (control plane)

Each tool invocation is authenticated via an MCP access token with a resource parameter bound to the UACN control plane's resource server identifier. The reasoning plane cannot invoke a tool outside the registered set, and cannot pass arguments that do not conform to the schema.

