Appendix B: TLA+ Vacuity Analysis and Counterexample Traces

B.1 The Vacuity Problem in Safety Model Checking

In model checking, an invariant is vacuously satisfied if its antecedent is never true in any reachable state. Vacuity detection is a recognized method for finding errors in the model-checking process when a specification is found to hold. A formula is non-vacuously satisfied if both the formula and its witness formula hold.

For UACN's model, the risk is that invariants with conditional antecedents — particularly INV-1 (phase = "SUBMITTING" => submitAuthValid) and INV-3 (auth.used = TRUE => phase \notin {"AUTHORIZED", "AWAITING_APPROVAL"}) — may hold vacuously if their antecedents are never reachable. The counterexample configurations below demonstrate non-vacuity by constructing weakened models in which the invariants can be falsified.

B.2 Counterexample 1: INV-1 Under Removed Guard

Weakened model. Remove AuthValid(auth) from the guard of Submit. Keep submitAuthValid' = AuthValid(auth) in the effect.

Trace. With MaxTime = 4, Checkout.currency = "USD", and the transition sequence:

1. ValidateIntent → Search → ReceiveOffers (now = 1) → Filter → Rank → RequestApproval → Approve (sets expires_at = now + 3 = 4).
2. TimeAdvance × 3 (now = 4).
3. At now = 4, now < auth.expires_at evaluates to 4 < 4 = FALSE, so AuthValid(auth) = FALSE.
4. Submit fires, setting phase = "SUBMITTING" and submitAuthValid = FALSE.

Result. INV-1 violated: submitAuthValid = FALSE while phase = "SUBMITTING". The trace length is 11 states.

B.3 Counterexample 2: INV-3 Under Resume Transition

Weakened model. Add:

```
Resume ==
    /\ phase \in {"CANCELLED", "FAILED"}
    /\ phase' = "AUTHORIZED"
    /\ UNCHANGED <<now, auth, attempt, submitted, providerStatus,
                    orderState, paymentState, compState, submitAuthValid>>
    /\ audit' = Append(audit, [t |-> now, ev |-> "resume"])
```

Trace. Full pre-submission sequence through Approve; Submit (sets auth.used = TRUE, phase = "SUBMITTING"); ProviderFail (sets phase = "FAILED"); Resume (sets phase = "AUTHORIZED" without resetting auth.used).

Result. INV-3 violated: auth.used = TRUE while phase = "AUTHORIZED". Trace length is 10 states.

B.4 Counterexample 3: INV-2 Under Re-Authorization

Weakened model. Add:

```
ReAuthorize ==
    /\ phase = "CONFIRMED"
    /\ phase' = "AUTHORIZED"
    /\ auth' = [ auth EXCEPT !.used = FALSE ]
    /\ UNCHANGED <<now, attempt, submitted, providerStatus,
                    orderState, paymentState, compState, submitAuthValid>>
    /\ audit' = Append(audit, [t |-> now, ev |-> "re_authorize"])
```

Trace. Full sequence through Approve; Submit (attempt.id = 1); ProviderConfirm (providerStatus[1] = "confirmed"); ReAuthorize (resets auth.used = FALSE); Submit (attempt.id = 2); ProviderConfirm (providerStatus[2] = "confirmed").

Result. INV-2 violated: Cardinality({i ∈ submitted : providerStatus[i] = "confirmed"}) = 2. Trace length is 12 states.

B.5 Vacuity Elimination Summary

Invariant Antecedent Reachable in base model? Non-vacuous proof
INV-1 phase = "SUBMITTING" Yes (via Submit) Counterexample 1
INV-2 Cardinality(...) > 1 No (structurally impossible) Counterexample 3
INV-3 auth.used = TRUE Yes (via Submit) Counterexample 2
INV-4 phase = "AMBIGUOUS" Yes (via ProviderTimeout) Environment action
INV-5 Len(audit) > 0 Yes (every transition) Timestamp monotonicity
INV-6 compState = "REFUND_CONFIRMED" Yes (via RefundConfirmed) Refund sequence
INV-7 orderState = "CANCELLED" Yes (via CancelConfirmed) Cancel sequence

