Appendix D: Context-Binding Nonce Cache and Consume-Once Enforcement

D.1 Nonce Cache Architecture

The nonce cache is a bounded, TTL-managed store of authorization nonces. Each entry has the form:

(n,\ \mathrm{ctx},\ \mathrm{state},\ \mathrm{expires\_at})

where n is the nonce, \mathrm{ctx} = H(m, h_c, P_{\mathrm{id}}, n), \mathrm{state} \in \{\mathrm{UNUSED}, \mathrm{CONSUMED}\}, and \mathrm{expires\_at} is the nonce TTL.

D.2 Consume-Once Operation

The consume-once operation is atomic:

```
CONSUME(n, ctx, now):
    entry = cache.lookup(n)
    if entry is null: return FAILURE
    if entry.expires_at < now: return FAILURE
    if entry.state = CONSUMED: return FAILURE
    if entry.ctx != ctx: return FAILURE
    entry.state = CONSUMED
    return SUCCESS
```

The operation is linearizable: concurrent consumers observe exactly one SUCCESS and all others FAILURE.

D.3 Runtime State Bound

The cache size is bounded by peak concurrency rather than history. Entries are evicted on TTL expiry or after consumption. The maximum cache size is:

|\mathrm{cache}|_{\max} = \lambda_{\max} \cdot T_{\mathrm{TTL}}

where \lambda_{\max} is the maximum authorization issuance rate and T_{\mathrm{TTL}} is the nonce time-to-live. This bound is independent of the total number of authorizations issued.

