Appendix I: Assumptions Register

I.1 Modelling Assumptions

A-1. The environment is nondeterministic: responses may be delayed, lost, duplicated, or conflicting. This assumption is modeled explicitly in the TLA+ specification.

A-2. The control plane is correct with respect to the tier separation. A compromised control plane (A5) violates this assumption.

A-3. The consumer's authenticator is honest. A compromised authenticator is out of scope.

A-4. The cryptographic primitives (SHA-256, ES256, WebAuthn, Ed25519) are secure. Quantum adversaries are out of scope.

A-5. The merchant's signature over the checkout is a valid attestation. A merchant that signs a checkout different from the one displayed to the consumer is a substitution attack; the model assumes the hash binds display to signature.

I.2 Design Assumptions

B-1. The canonical serialization is deterministic. Two implementations produce the same byte sequence for the same logical checkout.

B-2. The nonce cache has a TTL at least as long as the maximum authorization lifetime.

B-3. The WAL is durable: a write that returns success survives a crash.

B-4. The independent witness is append-only and not under the control plane's control.

I.3 Non-Assumptions

The following are explicitly not assumed:

· Merchant honesty.
· LLM reasoning integrity.
· Regulatory compliance.
· Production security.
· Universal exactly-once execution.
· Merchant capability-vector truthfulness.

These non-assumptions are the residual risks of the design.


