4. The Linearization Point and Consume-Once Semantics

```
   BEFORE t_ℓ                          AT t_ℓ (atomic)               AFTER t_ℓ
   ─────────                           ────────────────              ─────────

   ┌──────────────┐                 ┌──────────────────────┐      ┌──────────────────┐
   │ authorization│                 │  SINGLE DURABLE      │      │ authorization    │
   │ exists,      │                 │  TRANSACTION:        │      │ consumed;        │
   │ UNUSED       │  ──────────►    │                      │ ───► │ attempt durable; │
   │              │                 │  1. Verify(A)        │      │ SUBMITTING       │
   │ revocable    │                 │  2. Consume nonce n  │      │                  │
   │ safely       │                 │  3. Write SUBMITTING │      │ no window where  │
   │              │                 │     + attempt_id     │      │ verified-but-    │
   └──────────────┘                 └──────────────────────┘      │ not-consumed     │
                                                                   └──────────────────┘

   Verification predicate evaluated atomically at t_ℓ:
     Verify(σ_u, A) ∧ Verify(σ_m, C) ∧ H(C)=h_c ∧ Valid(A)
     ∧ ¬used(A) ∧ now < e

   Context binding (closes replay):
     ctx = H( merchant_id , checkout_hash , P_id , n )

   Crash-recovery semantics:
     crash before t_ℓ   →  auth not consumed; retry or revoke safely
     crash after t_ℓ,   →  outcome = UNCERTAIN; NO auto-retry;
        before send        human escalation
     crash after send   →  reconcile via (i, u, attempt_id, P_id^old)
```

