9. Logical Purchase Identity & Reconciliation

```
   P_id = H( u , i , h_c )

   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  Same (u, i, h_c)  ───►  same logical purchase  P_id         │
   │  Re-priced checkout ──►  NEW h_c ──► NEW P_id                │
   │                                                              │
   │  Rationale: a re-priced checkout is a different offer with   │
   │  different terms. Substituting terms under a stable identity │
   │  would be a bait-and-switch vector.                          │
   │                                                              │
   │  Consequence: P_id alone does not prevent double charge      │
   │  across a price change. Reconciliation key is (i, u), which  │
   │  IS stable across re-pricing.                                │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

   Reconciliation obligation:
        Ambiguous(o)  ⟹  Obligation( Reconcile(o) )

        query key: ( i , u , attempt_id , P_id^old )
        outcomes:  CONFIRMED | FAILED | UNKNOWN
        UNKNOWN after bounded attempts → human review

   Participant capability vector κ_m:
        ( κ_idem , κ_status , κ_stable_id , κ_pay_status , κ_recon ) ∈ {0,1}^5

        if κ_idem = 0 or κ_recon = 0:
             outcome := UNCERTAIN
             MUST NOT auto-retry

   Limitation: no orchestration layer guarantees exactly-once external
   execution across arbitrary merchants without cooperation or
   enforceable guarantees. Useful guarantee:
        one committed business effect per operation identity
        (weaker than exactly-once, stronger than at-most-once)
```

